---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/site/PageHero.astro";
import Flow from "../../components/diagrams/Flow.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../data/site";
import { breadcrumbLd } from "../../lib/library";

const trail = [
  { name: "Library", href: "/" },
  { name: "About", href: "/about/" },
  { name: "How we check facts", href: "/about/how-we-check/" }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How we check facts"
  description="Where PeopleCraft's legal and statutory facts come from, how often pages are re-checked, and what the 'Checked on' date on every page means."
  jsonLd={breadcrumbLd(Astro.site, trail)}
>
  <PageHero title="How we check facts" lede="Every rule, rate and date on PeopleCraft can be traced to a source you can open yourself. Here is how that works." trail={trail} />

  <div class="wrap-narrow section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where facts come from</h2>
      <p>We use sources in this order and stop at the first that settles the question:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The law itself:</strong> the Act or Code as published on India Code, and the rules and notifications in the Gazette of India or a state gazette.</li>
        <li><strong>Official explanations:</strong> Press Information Bureau releases, and FAQs, circulars and forms from the Ministry of Labour and Employment, EPFO, ESIC, the Income Tax Department and state labour or tax departments.</li>
        <li><strong>Court judgments</strong> where a court has settled how a rule applies.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reputable secondary sources</strong> (law and accounting firms, PRS Legislative Research, legal news services) when the official text isn't available online. Where a number rests
          only on secondary sources, the page says "secondary source only" next to it, so you know to confirm it with the department.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Each chapter and tool lists its sources at the bottom of the page.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="flowfig">
      <Flow
        steps={[
          { title: "Find the rule", detail: "Act, rules, notification" },
          { title: "Check the latest change", detail: "Gazette, PIB, department notices" },
          { title: "Write it plainly", detail: "One idea per sentence, with an example" },
          { title: "Test the numbers", detail: "Worked examples for every calculator" },
          { title: "Date the page", detail: "Checked-on date and sources list" }
        ]}
        label="The checking process, from finding the rule to dating the page"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What the date on each page means</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>"Sources checked"</strong> is the last day the facts on that page were compared with the sources listed at its foot. It is not the date the page was written. If a page hasn't been checked in a
        while, treat its numbers with more care.
      </p>
      <p>
        PeopleCraft is written by one person, and the first drafts were researched and written with AI assistance (see the <a href="/about/">About page</a>). Each page was then checked against its sources,
        but no lawyer or chartered accountant has reviewed it yet. Until one does, the stamp on the page says <strong>"Expert review pending"</strong>. When a qualified reviewer signs a page off, the stamp
        shows their name and the date instead.
      </p>

      <h2>How often pages are checked</h2>
      <p>
        The aim is to re-check every page {SITE.reviewCadence}. Every build of the site warns when a page's date is more than 100 days old, so a page can't quietly go
        stale.
      </p>

      <h2>One number, one place</h2>
      <p>
        Statutory figures such as the EPF wage ceiling, the ESI limit or the gratuity cap are stored once. Calculators read them from that one file, and chapters and the glossary show them from it. A check
        that runs on every build fails if a page types one of these figures by hand. Worked examples and dated history ("the ceiling rose to ₹25,000 on 17 September 2026") are listed separately, and
        are flagged for a rewrite when a figure changes.
      </p>

      <h2>When the law isn't settled</h2>
      <p>
        Some questions don't have an official answer yet, for example exactly when the two-working-day clock for final wages starts. We say so on the page, explain the common reading and don't present it as
        certain. We would rather tell you a question is open than guess.
      </p>

      <h2>Calculators</h2>
      <p>
        Every calculator shows its formula, the date its figures were checked and the law it follows. Each one is tested against worked examples before it is published. Calculators give the statutory minimum;
        your contract or company policy may give you more.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If you think something is wrong, tell us through the links on the <a href="/about/">About page</a>. We check the report against the source, fix the page and update its "Sources checked" date.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
    padding-bottom: var(--section);
  }
  .flowfig {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--canvas);
  }
</style>
